WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.473
People say thread as if you already know what one is.

2
00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.601
Is it your code?

3
00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:08.589
The processor?

4
00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:10.141
Some object in memory?

5
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:13.370
If you have never been quite sure, you are not alone.

6
00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.317
Let's find out, with a small shop.

7
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.202
The shop records every sale.

8
00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:22.221
At the end of the year, the owner wants one number: the total of a million sales.

9
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.244
Our program adds them up, one after another.

10
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.441
The owner's laptop has four cores.

11
00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:33.264
A core is the part of the processor that does the work, and all four can work at the same moment.

12
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:37.292
But while our program adds, one core works, and three sit idle.

13
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:41.996
It's like paying four clerks, and letting one do all the adding while three watch.

14
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:46.172
So why not cut the list into four, and add all four parts at once?

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.184
Before computers, a shop gave this job to people.

16
00:00:50.184 --> 00:00:52.133
Picture clerks in a back room.

17
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.073
The receipts sit in one filing cabinet that everyone shares.

18
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:59.559
Each clerk keeps a running total on a notepad of their own.

19
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:00.634
Let's map it.

20
00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:07.981
Each clerk is a thread: one path of work that follows the steps, one by one, and always knows where it is.

21
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.010
The filing cabinet is the heap.

22
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.611
Every object you create with new lives there, and every thread can reach it.

23
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.960
The notepad is the thread's stack: its local variables, and the method calls it is in the middle of.

24
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.685
Only its owner writes on it.

25
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:28.249
And the whole back room is the process: one running program, with memory of its own.

26
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.219
Quick guess.

27
00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:32.424
One clerk writes total equals 3 on her notepad.

28
00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:34.948
Does the next clerk's notepad change?

29
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.019
No.

30
00:01:38.019 --> 00:01:41.589
A local variable lives on its own thread's stack.

31
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:45.606
But both clerks can open the same drawer and reach the same sale.

32
00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:49.597
That is what makes threads useful: they share data with no copying.

33
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:55.457
It is also what makes them risky, because two threads can change one object at the same moment.

34
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.783
So, locals belong to one thread.

35
00:01:57.783 --> 00:01:59.965
The heap belongs to all of them.

36
00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:04.162
Before we hire anyone, one more surprise.

37
00:02:04.162 --> 00:02:07.646
How many threads does the emptiest Java program have?

38
00:02:07.646 --> 00:02:08.697
Pause and guess.

39
00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:17.694
The lesson's first demo asks the JVM, the program that runs your Java code.

40
00:02:17.694 --> 00:02:19.995
On JDK 21, the list has six.

41
00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:23.637
One is main, the thread that runs your main method.

42
00:02:23.637 --> 00:02:30.301
The other five are the JVM's housekeepers, mostly cleaning up after objects nobody uses any more.

43
00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:33.291
So your programs had several threads all along.

44
00:02:33.291 --> 00:02:35.515
You just never met the cleaning crew.

45
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:39.319
Now let's hire four clerks.

46
00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.363
We make four Thread objects, named sales-worker-0 to 3.

47
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:52.628
Each one gets work to do: a lambda that sums one slice of the sales, and puts the result in its own slot of an array, here in the cabinet.

48
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.759
Then we call run() on each one.

49
00:02:54.759 --> 00:02:56.844
Which thread will sum each slice?

50
00:02:56.844 --> 00:02:57.855
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:03.692
We printed the thread's name inside the work.

52
00:03:03.692 --> 00:03:05.843
Every line says: summed on main.

53
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:11.061
The total is right, the tests pass, and it is exactly as slow as before.

54
00:03:11.061 --> 00:03:13.251
Main did all four slices itself.

55
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.107
Here's why.

56
00:03:14.107 --> 00:03:20.020
run() is an ordinary method call, so the work runs right here, on the thread that called it.

57
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.233
Named honestly, it would be called do it yourself.

58
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.999
The call that makes a thread is start().

59
00:03:25.999 --> 00:03:31.456
The JVM asks the operating system for a new thread, and that new thread calls run().

60
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.470
So we change run() to start(), and add up the four slots right after the loop.

61
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.907
Is the total right now?

62
00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.102
The lesson ran it five times.

63
00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:48.688
The first total came out at about a quarter of the right answer, and the other four at about three quarters.

64
00:03:48.933 --> 00:03:52.342
Main added up the slots while the clerks were still adding.

65
00:03:52.342 --> 00:03:53.960
Call it the impatient total.

66
00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:58.705
You might be thinking: just sleep for a hundred milliseconds, to give them time.

67
00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:03.243
That passes on your laptop, and fails one day on a busier computer.

68
00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:04.916
Sleep only lets time pass.

69
00:04:04.916 --> 00:04:07.039
It's a guess with a number on it.

70
00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.865
What we want is to wait for each clerk.

71
00:04:09.865 --> 00:04:10.864
That is join().

72
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.619
t.join() makes the caller wait until thread t has ended.

73
00:04:14.619 --> 00:04:18.885
And once it returns, the caller sees everything that thread wrote.

74
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:24.333
So main starts all four, then joins all four, and only then adds up the slots.

75
00:04:24.333 --> 00:04:26.696
Now the total is right, every time.

76
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.923
Here it is in Java and in Kotlin.

77
00:04:30.923 --> 00:04:32.402
Notice the two loops.

78
00:04:32.402 --> 00:04:35.852
Start every worker first, then join every worker.

79
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.662
Join inside the first loop, and main would wait for worker 0 before starting worker 1.

80
00:04:41.662 --> 00:04:43.373
One clerk at a time again.

81
00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:49.748
In Kotlin, thread with braces makes the thread and starts it for you, so the run() mistake is hard to make.

82
00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:51.978
Does it pay?

83
00:04:51.978 --> 00:05:02.253
On a bigger job of the same shape, on four cores, the lesson's timing demo took 510 ms on one thread, and 121 on four.

84
00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:04.704
So, what is a thread?

85
00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:12.797
One clerk: a path of work that follows your code step by step, with its own notepad, sharing one cabinet with everyone else.

86
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.319
Start every worker, then join every worker, and read nothing before the last join.

87
00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:20.649
Check yourself.

88
00:05:20.649 --> 00:05:26.737
A friend splits a slow report into four parts, and calls run() on a new thread for each part.

89
00:05:26.737 --> 00:05:29.159
On four cores, how much faster is it?

90
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.251
Not faster at all.

91
00:05:33.251 --> 00:05:37.283
run() is a plain call, so main does all four parts itself.
